		{
			"id": "zkT30SqC",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -402.93493603344444,
			"y": 4470.231498246563,
			"width": 1077.9986572265625,
			"height": 375,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 1,
			"strokeStyle": "dashed",
			"roughness": 0,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b0N",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1924316158,
			"version": 227,
			"versionNonce": 2030973886,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1783640619576,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "If the Adjacency Matrix is so fast, why don't we use it for everything?\n\nBecause of wasted space.\n\nImagine you are programming Facebook. Facebook has roughly 3 billion users.If you build an\nAdjacency Matrix for Facebook, your 2D array must have 3 billion rows and 3 billion\ncolumns.3,000,000,000 x 3,000,000,000 = 9 quintillion boxes.\n\nHere is the fatal flaw: The average Facebook user only has about 300 friends. That means out of\nthe 3 billion boxes in a user's row, exactly 300 of them will contain a 1. The other 2,999,999,700\nboxes will contain a 0.\n\nYour computer's RAM is now completely full of billions of useless zeroes, representing friendships\nthat do not exist. In computer science, a network with a ton of nodes but very few connections is\ncalled a Sparse Graph.",
			"rawText": "If the Adjacency Matrix is so fast, why don't we use it for everything?\n\nBecause of wasted space.\n\nImagine you are programming Facebook. Facebook has roughly 3 billion users.If you build an Adjacency Matrix for Facebook, your 2D array must have 3 billion rows and 3 billion columns.3,000,000,000 x 3,000,000,000 = 9 quintillion boxes.\n\nHere is the fatal flaw: The average Facebook user only has about 300 friends. That means out of the 3 billion boxes in a user's row, exactly 300 of them will contain a 1. The other 2,999,999,700 boxes will contain a 0.\n\nYour computer's RAM is now completely full of billions of useless zeroes, representing friendships that do not exist. In computer science, a network with a ton of nodes but very few connections is called a Sparse Graph.",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 8,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": "FSkmf6uc",
			"originalText": "If the Adjacency Matrix is so fast, why don't we use it for everything?\n\nBecause of wasted space.\n\nImagine you are programming Facebook. Facebook has roughly 3 billion users.If you build an Adjacency Matrix for Facebook, your 2D array must have 3 billion rows and 3 billion columns.3,000,000,000 x 3,000,000,000 = 9 quintillion boxes.\n\nHere is the fatal flaw: The average Facebook user only has about 300 friends. That means out of the 3 billion boxes in a user's row, exactly 300 of them will contain a 1. The other 2,999,999,700 boxes will contain a 0.\n\nYour computer's RAM is now completely full of billions of useless zeroes, representing friendships that do not exist. In computer science, a network with a ton of nodes but very few connections is called a Sparse Graph.",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "Onpx34o9",
			"type": "text",
