		{
			"id": "BdNs7hol",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -407.0341387744959,
			"y": 4219.409724882865,
			"width": 953.3239038384622,
			"height": 25,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "dashed",
			"roughness": 0,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b0R",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 491334754,
			"version": 144,
			"versionNonce": 2005326910,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1783640619576,
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "The primary limitation of the Adjacency Matrix arises when dealing with Sparse Graphs.",
			"rawText": "The primary limitation of the Adjacency Matrix arises when dealing with Sparse Graphs.",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 8,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "The primary limitation of the Adjacency Matrix arises when dealing with Sparse Graphs.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
		{
			"id": "gO0nGaog",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -407.76514315926795,
			"y": 4256.090537775781,
			"width": 1084.4298809259872,
			"height": 75,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "dashed",
			"roughness": 0,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b0S",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1347602274,
			"version": 92,
			"versionNonce": 1309839010,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1783640619576,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "In graph theory, a sparse graph is a network where the number of actual edges (E) is significantly\nsmaller than the maximum possible number of edges (V^2). Most real-world networks—such as routing\ntopologies, social graphs, and web link structures—are highly sparse.",
			"rawText": "In graph theory, a sparse graph is a network where the number of actual edges (E) is significantly smaller than the maximum possible number of edges (V^2). Most real-world networks—such as routing topologies, social graphs, and web link structures—are highly sparse.",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 8,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "In graph theory, a sparse graph is a network where the number of actual edges (E) is significantly smaller than the maximum possible number of edges (V^2). Most real-world networks—such as routing topologies, social graphs, and web link structures—are highly sparse.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "5gbmCkli",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -406.2261192172067,
			"y": 4357.451986419703,
			"width": 1075.4061556982856,
			"height": 75,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "dashed",
			"roughness": 0,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b0V",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1761390142,
			"version": 155,
			"versionNonce": 1025287294,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1783640619576,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "When a sparse graph is stored in an Adjacency Matrix, the O(V^2) memory allocation results in the\nstorage of millions of 0s. This consumes vast amounts of contiguous memory to represent non-\nexistent connections, creating severe scalability bottlenecks for large datasets.",
			"rawText": "When a sparse graph is stored in an Adjacency Matrix, the O(V^2) memory allocation results in the storage of millions of 0s. This consumes vast amounts of contiguous memory to represent non-existent connections, creating severe scalability bottlenecks for large datasets.",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 8,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "When a sparse graph is stored in an Adjacency Matrix, the O(V^2) memory allocation results in the storage of millions of 0s. This consumes vast amounts of contiguous memory to represent non-existent connections, creating severe scalability bottlenecks for large datasets.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
